[34m##[0m[37m [0m[34mHow the polyglot works[0m

[37mThe[0m[37m file is split into two regions[0m[37m, each hidden from[0m[37m the [0m[35m*[0m[35mother[0m[35m*[0m[37m language:[0m

[1;33m**[0m[1;33mLines 1–3 — hide the C code from Python[0m[1;33m**[0m
[37m```[0m
[34m#[0m[34mif[0m[37m 0[0m
[37m""""[0m
[34m#[0m[34mendif[0m
[37m```[0m
[37m- *Python*: `#if 0` is a comment, then `""""` opens a triple-quoted string (contents start with the 4th `"`).[0m
[37m- *C*: `#if 0` skips to `#endif`, so the quotes are never seen as tokens.[0m

[37m**Line 49 — close the Python string while staying invisible to C**[0m
[37m```[0m
[37m/[0m[35m*[0m[35m"""#[0m[35m*[0m[37m/[0m
[37m```[0m
[37m- *Python*: it's still inside the string, so `/*` is just content; the `"""` terminates the string; `#*/` is then a comment.[0m
[37m- *C*: it's one complete `/* ... */` comment — the quotes vanish in translation phase 3, so the preprocessor never sees an odd quote.[0m

[37m(Using `"""` here instead of `/*"""#*/` produces the harmless but ugly *"missing terminating `"` character"* warning; this form avoids it entirely. The 4-quote opener is for the same reason — an odd quote count inside the skipped block triggers that warning.)[0m

[37m**Lines 50–70 — hide the Python code from C** with a plain `#if 0 … #endif` (both lines are Python comments).[0m

[37m## Implementation notes[0m

[37m- **Python**: iterative `f(0)=0, f(1)=1` loop with arbitrary-precision ints.[0m
[37m- **C**: no 64-bit ceiling — a base-10⁹ bignum (`unsigned int` limbs, 20000 limbs ≈ 180k digits) so the C output matches Python's exactly for any input, not just `n ≤ 93`. Limb arrays are rotated by pointer, no copying.[0m

